$DIR/main.p:17:10: hits is read here without an atomic operation, but other tasks change it with atomic operations
    Every write to hits that another task may run at the same time holds atomic
    operations: main.p:7 in handle (the task started by task handle at
    main.p:13). So the compiler infers that atomic operations guards hits, and
    every read must hold it too: a read without it may run while a write is half
    done.
    Here the main task reads it at main.p:17 in main with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Read it with the atomic type's Load method, as the writes use atomic
    operations
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
